"""Patient questions matched to the legal issue explained by each glossary term."""

QUESTIONS = {
    "Medical Malpractice": ("Does an unsuccessful treatment establish malpractice?", "No. The review must identify the relevant duty, what the provider did, the harm and the connection between them. Consent and the response to a complication can require assessment even when the procedure itself was performed appropriately."),
    "Health Law": ("Which legal rules apply to my treatment?", "Start with the provider's identity and whether treatment was public or private. Patient rights, contracts, consumer procedure, administrative responsibility and data protection may address different parts of the same event."),
    "Informed Consent": ("Does a signed form prove I received an adequate explanation?", "A signature is evidence of signing. The form, consultation notes, language, timing and opportunity to ask questions help show whether the explanation supported an informed decision."),
    "Statute of Limitations": ("Which dates does a lawyer need to calculate my deadline?", "Provide treatment and discharge dates, when you discovered the harm and its possible cause, the provider's identity and status, and all prior applications or proceedings. Different claims can have different periods and start dates; do not assume a complaint pauses them."),
    "Pecuniary Damages": ("Is every expense after treatment recoverable?", "An invoice shows a cost, but its connection to the alleged wrong, necessity and reasonable amount still need assessment. Keep proof of payment and medical explanations for further treatment, as well as any refunds or insurance payments."),
    "Non-Pecuniary Damages": ("Is there a fixed payment for pain or distress?", "There is no universal amount for a procedure or complication. The court considers the established event and its consequences under the applicable rules. Financial losses require a separate calculation."),
    "Complication": ("Can a recognised complication still raise a legal concern?", "Yes. A recognised risk may occur without negligence, but information, monitoring and the response to warning signs still matter. Compare the symptom timeline with what the provider knew and did."),
    "Power of Attorney": ("Can I authorise a Turkish lawyer while living abroad?", "Often, but the required wording and execution route must be confirmed first. A Turkish consulate or an appropriately formalised foreign notarial document may be relevant. Ask the lawyer for the required authority before signing; translation, apostille or other formalities depend on the document and jurisdiction."),
    "Court of Cassation": ("Does a reversal mean the patient has received compensation?", "No. A reversal can require the lower court to reconsider evidence, procedure or damages. Read the operative result and later proceedings before describing a decision as a final award or a payment."),
    "Forensic Medicine Institute": ("Can an expert report be challenged?", "The report should address the referral questions using the relevant records and expertise. Missing inputs, unexplained conclusions or conflicting opinions may justify a procedural request for clarification or further review. The applicable court procedure determines the available step."),
    "Burden of Proof": ("Does the patient have to prove every disputed fact?", "Allocation depends on the proposition and legal basis. Separate the alleged duty, consent process, breach, injury, causation and financial loss. Identify which records each party holds rather than assuming the burden is the same for all issues."),
    "Health Tourism": ("Is the booking agency necessarily responsible for clinical treatment?", "Responsibility depends on the agency's actual role, undertaking and governing rules. Identify who advertised, contracted, received payment, operated and provided follow-up. A shared brand alone does not establish that every participant has the same obligations."),
    "Duty of Care": ("Does the provider's responsibility end at discharge?", "The review can include discharge advice, monitoring, referral and the response to reported symptoms. What follow-up was required depends on the treatment and circumstances; an unanswered message alone does not establish a breach or causation."),
    "Turkish Code of Obligations": ("Does one limitation rule cover every medical claim?", "No. Contract, tort and defective-performance provisions have different requirements. The treatment relationship, requested remedy and relevant dates must be identified before choosing a provision."),
    "Mediation": ("Must every medical dispute go through mediation?", "Requirements depend on the claim, parties and competent route. Some consumer or commercial claims involve a pre-action mediation requirement, while a public-provider dispute may follow a different process. Confirm the applicable requirement and deadline before filing."),
    "Criminal Liability": ("Does a compensation claim automatically establish a crime?", "No. A criminal investigation requires assessment of an offence and its elements under criminal procedure. Civil compensation, professional discipline and criminal responsibility are separate questions, even when some evidence overlaps."),
    "Standard of Care": ("Is a poor result enough to show the standard was breached?", "Compare the provider's decisions with appropriate practice in the circumstances at the time, including the patient's risks and available information. A later outcome does not by itself establish what should have been done earlier."),
    "Consumer Law": ("Does every hospital dispute belong in a consumer court?", "No. The provider's public or private status, parties and transaction matter. Identify the actual contracting entities and requested remedy before selecting a court or assuming mediation is required."),
    "Medical Board Report": ("Does a report settle all the issues in my claim?", "A report may address only diagnosis, impairment or another limited question. Check the board's mandate, specialties, records and reasoning. A finding about disability does not necessarily decide fault, causation or damages."),
    "Causation": ("Does a problem appearing after surgery prove surgery caused it?", "Timing is relevant, but alternative causes and the patient's earlier condition also need review. Clinical records and suitable expert reasoning should explain the link between the specific conduct challenged and the specific harm."),
    "Permanent Disability Rate": ("Does a disability percentage determine my compensation?", "It is one possible input, not the entire valuation. The assessment method, permanence, causal connection, age, work and other proven losses may matter. Check which regulation and purpose the report uses."),
    "Temporary Incapacity": ("How can I show that recovery prevented me from working?", "Keep dated medical restrictions, leave records, employment or business records and evidence of income. A recovery period and a financial loss are related but distinct facts; document both."),
    "Iatrogenic Injury": ("Does treatment-related injury necessarily mean negligence?", "No. The term describes the relationship to medical treatment. The legal review separately examines care, information, preventability, complication management and causation."),
    "Nosocomial Infection": ("Does an infection acquired in hospital prove negligent hygiene?", "Not by itself. Timing, microbiology, patient risks, prevention measures and the clinical response require review. Preserve test results and treatment records without assuming the source or a particular failure."),
    "Wrong-Site Surgery": ("Which records help establish what procedure was authorised?", "Compare the planned procedure, consent, site marking, safety checks and operative record. Record any difference between the authorised site or intervention and what occurred, and obtain independent assessment of the consequences."),
    "Surgical Negligence": ("Can photographs establish a technical surgical error?", "Photographs may document appearance and timing, but operative notes, earlier condition and specialist assessment are usually needed to evaluate technique. Keep original dated files and avoid treating appearance alone as a diagnosis."),
    "Post-Operative Care": ("What should I preserve if the clinic stops answering?", "Keep discharge instructions, follow-up arrangements, full messages, symptom dates, call attempts and any independent care records. Failed contact may be relevant to follow-up obligations, but does not by itself prove abandonment or fault."),
    "Off-Label Drug Use": ("Is use outside a medicine's authorised indication automatically unlawful?", "The indication, applicable rules, clinical justification, approvals where required, information and monitoring need assessment. Record the exact medicine, dose, purpose and prescribing professional rather than relying on the label alone."),
    "Medical Device Liability": ("Could responsibility involve someone other than the surgeon?", "Depending on the alleged problem, the manufacturer, supplier, facility or treating professional may have different roles. Preserve the model, serial or lot number, implant card and instructions so a product issue can be distinguished from selection or use."),
    "Cosmetic Surgery Regulation": ("Does a clinic's authorisation guarantee a safe result?", "Authorisation concerns permission and regulatory requirements. It does not resolve whether an individual procedure met the applicable standard or whether consent was adequate. Check both the authorisation at the treatment date and the care actually provided."),
    "Loss of Earnings": ("How are future income losses supported?", "The calculation needs evidence of prior earnings, the medical effect on work and realistic assumptions about the future. Employment, tax and business records help distinguish demonstrated loss from an estimate based only on the patient's concern."),
    "Future Medical Expenses": ("Can I include treatment that has not happened yet?", "A medically supported plan and credible cost information can help assess future needs. Explain why the treatment is necessary and connected to the harm; an unqualified quote alone does not establish recoverability."),
    "Loss of Consortium": ("Is this English label a separate Turkish damages claim?", "Do not assume an English-law category maps directly onto Turkish law. The affected person's relationship, personal harm and the applicable Turkish provisions must be reviewed to identify any available claim."),
    "Wrongful Death": ("Do all relatives have the same claim after a patient's death?", "No. Support loss, funeral expenses and non-financial harm have distinct conditions. The relationship, financial dependency, causal evidence and requested remedy need individual assessment."),
    "Bereavement Damages": ("Is there a fixed bereavement award in Turkish law?", "Do not import a fixed award from another jurisdiction. The relevant Turkish non-pecuniary damages rules, claimant's relationship and established circumstances govern the assessment."),
    "Damage Mitigation Obligation": ("Must I accept the original clinic's revision offer?", "An offer should be assessed for medical suitability, risk and any settlement or release terms. Reasonable recovery decisions matter, but a clinic's offer alone does not decide what the patient must accept. Keep independent advice and the offer in full."),
    "Interest on Damages": ("Does interest always start on the treatment date?", "The claim, defendant, default and governing rules can affect the start date and rate. Keep payment, demand, notification and filing dates so the interest request can be calculated under the relevant route."),
    "Lump Sum vs. Periodic Payment": ("Can I assume compensation will be paid as a lump sum?", "The applicable remedy, court decision or settlement determines payment structure. Future loss assumptions and any proposed instalments should be reviewed before agreeing to payment terms."),
    "Malpractice Insurance": ("Does a doctor's policy guarantee payment of my claim?", "No. Liability and policy coverage are separate. Insured status, policy period, exclusions, limits and notification requirements require examination. A court award also needs to be distinguished from collection."),
    "Social Security Recourse": ("Is the social security institution's recovery the same as my compensation?", "No. An institution's recovery of benefits or treatment costs can involve a separate legal claim. Identify benefits and payments so their treatment under the patient's own damages calculation can be reviewed."),
    "Contractual Liability": ("Does a clinic's advertisement form part of its undertaking?", "Advertisements, messages, signed terms and consultations can help establish what was represented or agreed. Their legal effect depends on the relationship and applicable rules; preserve the original wording and date."),
    "Tortious Liability": ("Can a claim exist without a written treatment contract?", "Possibly. A tort claim has its own requirements, including the alleged wrongful conduct, injury and causal connection. The absence of a signed contract does not by itself decide either liability or the available route."),
    "Vicarious Liability": ("Is a hospital automatically responsible for every doctor using its premises?", "The doctor's role, employment or contractual arrangement, the hospital's undertaking and governing rules matter. Facility branding alone is insufficient to identify the legal relationship."),
    "Joint and Several Liability": ("Can I claim the full loss from every participant in the treatment?", "Only where the applicable rules and established facts support that form of responsibility. Identify each participant's role and causal contribution; naming several parties does not itself establish joint liability."),
    "Strict Liability": ("Does a serious injury mean I do not need to establish fault?", "No. A specific legal basis is needed for liability without fault. Severity of harm is not a substitute for identifying that basis and proving its remaining requirements."),
    "Contributory Negligence": ("Can the provider simply blame me for the complication?", "An allegation about smoking, missed follow-up or other conduct must be examined against instructions, records and causal evidence. Patient conduct may affect assessment, but it does not automatically excuse deficient care."),
    "Force Majeure": ("Does an unexpected event automatically excuse the clinic?", "No. The event, foreseeability, control, contractual duties and causal effect require assessment under the governing rules. An ordinary clinical risk should not be labelled force majeure without that analysis."),
    "Assumption of Risk": ("Did agreeing to a surgical risk waive my right to proper care?", "Consent to a disclosed risk is different from accepting negligent treatment. The information provided and whether the provider met the relevant duties still need separate review."),
    "Breach of Contract": ("Does disappointment with the result prove a breach?", "Compare the actual undertaking with performance, clinical limitations and the evidence. A disagreement about appearance alone does not establish what was promised or which contractual remedy applies."),
    "Medical Service Contract": ("Is every elective treatment a contract guaranteeing a result?", "No. Classification depends on the actual treatment purpose and undertaking. Therapeutic care, an aesthetic procedure and facility services can raise different contractual questions; a marketing label alone does not resolve them."),
    "Patient Rights Regulation": ("Is a patient-rights complaint the same as a compensation claim?", "No. A complaint may address information, access, dignity or institutional practice. Compensation requires the relevant legal route and evidence of its conditions. Check deadlines separately rather than assuming a complaint preserves a claim."),
    "Right to Refuse Treatment": ("Can I change my decision after agreeing to treatment?", "The patient's informed and voluntary decision matters, but timing, capacity, statutory exceptions and treatment already performed may affect the available steps. Ask the provider to explain the consequences and document the decision."),
    "Right to Second Opinion": ("Can an overseas doctor's opinion help a Turkish claim?", "It can document current findings and treatment needs. Preserve the full report and source records. Translation and procedural requirements may apply, and the Turkish court may require its own expert assessment."),
    "Privacy of Medical Data": ("Is explicit consent the only possible basis for processing health data?", "No. Current KVKK Article 6 includes other specified conditions, including processing necessary to establish, exercise or protect a right. The purpose, applicable condition, safeguards and any transfer must be assessed separately."),
    "Right to Access Medical Records": ("What can I do if a clinic does not provide my records?", "Make an identifiable written request listing the relevant treatment and documents, and retain delivery evidence and responses. Access procedures and protection of other people's information may matter. Advice can identify an appropriate complaint or judicial request if access remains unresolved."),
    "Patient Advocate": ("Can a patient advocate act as my lawyer in court?", "Support with communication or records does not by itself confer authority to represent a person in legal proceedings. Confirm the advocate's role, qualifications and authorisation, and distinguish practical support from licensed legal representation."),
    "Vulnerable Patient": ("Does vulnerability automatically mean the patient cannot consent?", "No. Language, disability, age or dependence can create support needs without removing decision-making capacity. Assess the individual patient's understanding and voluntariness, with appropriate assistance."),
    "Minor Patient Consent": ("Can a parent’s signature resolve every consent question?", "The child's age, understanding, representation, intervention and applicable rules matter. The child's involvement and any disagreement should be assessed rather than assuming one signature settles the process."),
    "Capacity to Consent": ("Does a diagnosis alone determine capacity?", "Capacity concerns the relevant decision and circumstances. Understanding, ability to evaluate information and communicate a voluntary choice require assessment; a diagnostic label alone does not answer the legal question."),
    "Emergency Exception to Consent": ("Does urgency remove all consent duties?", "An exception must be assessed against the immediate circumstances and applicable rules. Record the threat, available time, patient's capacity, attempts to obtain a decision and the intervention necessary. Routine scheduling pressure is not itself an emergency."),
}
